00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
and they're you want to treat them with respect and things.

253
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
So I asked one of the fellas who is on the camp staff,

254
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
who was down in the site with us. it's one of our scouts.

255
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,000
I said you know, go on up, find the nature director,

256
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
tell them we got a snake

257
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and tell them to bring the snake stick and stuff down there.

258
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
You know they've got a, you know we wait about 10 or 15 minutes

259
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:04,000
and the nature director comes running breathlessly into the campsite

260
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
and in rather a louder voice than I would have hoped,

261
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
he talks about the copperhead

262
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
and we've got to capture this copperhead

263
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and I'm like, yeah, well, let's, we might be able to do this

264
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
without causing a whole lot of craziness

265
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
and let's just keep it quiet. oh, oh, yeah, okay.

266
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
So we begin trying to kind of, you know,

267
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
reach under the platform and get the snake.

268
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
and of course the snake is not very interested in this.

269
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
the snake would rather stay where he's at,

270
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
he's comfortable. he's got a couple of scouts right there,

271
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:44,000
should he need to bite them, poison them for any reason.

272
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
so he's really got a pretty good crib right there

273
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,000
and he's not very interested in moving.

274
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
one thing leads to another right,

275
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and finally there's, you know, five adults and scouts

276
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,000
and 10 flashlights and there's a lot of hollering.

277
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,000
so our quiet little snake extraction turns into, you know,

278
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
a major operation

279
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and we've completely disassembled the tent platform

280
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
to get at this snake

281
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and finally get it in the snake stick

282
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
after a whole lot of excitement.

283
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and so me and the nature director

284
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:24,000
walk on down the trail to a remote part of camp

285
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:28,000
to release the snake and just, you know, let him be,

286
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
he'll be out there where he won't bother anybody.

287
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
and so I thought, you know, great end of story.

288
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
the boys won't be concerned about this copperhead.

289
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
of course they'll be concerned that there is a copperhead now

290
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
under every tent platform,

291
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
because we didn't manage to do it very quietly

292
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
but we all headed off to bed

293
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:50,000
and I had kind of figured that we were done.

294
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:57,000
but we weren't.

295
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,000
no, of course we weren't.

296
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
you know, one of the great things about being in camp with the scouts

297
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
is that any little piece of news travels very quickly

298
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
in camp. we call it the moccasin telegraph.

299
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
pretty soon the whole camp knows

300
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and then the story gets embroidered and embellished.

301
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,000
so here we are, we're walking back from lunch the next day.

302
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
I get back to the campsite

303
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and one of my assistant leaders catches up with me

304
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and he says yeah, he says

305
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
there's quite a story about the snake last night.

306
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and I say really.

307
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
he says yeah, well, this is the story right now,

308
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
because as he was walking back from lunch

309
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
he heard scouts from another troop discussing this.

310
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:44,000
so the story was that there was this huge copperhead

311
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
coiled up on the bed-

312
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
not under the platform but on the bed

313
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
of one of the young scouts in our troop-

314
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
and it was attacking them

315
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
and the scoutmaster had to kill it with an axe

316
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and then had apparently skin that made a hatband

317
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
to be sporting at that night's retreat ceremony.

318
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
so you know, that's the way the moccasin telegraph works, isn't it?

319
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:15,000
and it has tempted me before to create some stories,

320
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
but I've managed to resist the temptation.

